= Mandioquinha

_Scope: This entry treats_ mandioquinha _(Brazilian Portuguese) — also known as_ batata-baroa _(southeastern Brazil), arracacha (Andean Spanish), Peruvian carrot, Peruvian parsnip, white carrot —_ Arracacia xanthorrhiza _Bancroft (Apiaceae). The plant is an_ Andean pre-Columbian domesticate, _native to the cool-mountain regions of Colombia, Ecuador, Peru, Bolivia, and Venezuela, with substantial post-Columbian spread into Brazilian highland agriculture (especially the Minas Gerais and southeast-Brazilian region). Status:_ not classical _in any Old World tradition — Ayurveda, Tibb, Greco-Roman, or Chinese. The classical and continuous tradition is_ Andean indigenous _(Quechua, Aymara) and_ modern Brazilian. _Importantly, mandioquinha is_ not _the same plant as_ mandioca / cassava _(_Manihot esculenta_, Euphorbiaceae — see eventual_ mandioca.typ _entry) — the diminutive name "little mandioca" is a folk-naming confusion, since the plants are botanically unrelated despite the superficial root resemblance. The leaves and seeds also have ethnobotanical use in indigenous Andean medicine._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin / botanical:* _Arracacia xanthorrhiza_ Bancroft (1825), family Apiaceae (Umbelliferae). The plant is in the same family as carrot, parsnip, fennel, parsley, celery — and indeed botanically *closer to carrot than to cassava* (despite the Brazilian "mandioquinha" naming). The species epithet *xanthorrhiza* = Greek ξανθός "yellow" + ῥίζα "root," for the characteristic yellow-orange interior of the principal cultivated variety. Genus name *Arracacia* from the Quechua / Spanish *arracacha*. The plant produces a *crown of cylindrical fleshy roots* growing from a central thickened stem (the *cepa*); the *fleshy roots are the edible food*, with the central stem typically replanted to propagate the next crop.

*Brazilian Portuguese:* *Mandioquinha* — "little mandioca / little cassava" — the diminutive of _mandioca_; a *folk-naming confusion* (the plant is taxonomically distinct from cassava, but the small cylindrical root shape and starchy texture led to the diminutive analogy in southeastern Brazilian usage). Also *mandioquinha-salsa* — "parsley-mandioquinha" — for the parsley-like aerial parts (the Apiaceae botany is clear in the leaf morphology). *Batata-baroa* — "baroa potato" (origin of "baroa" unclear; possibly from a regional Italian-Brazilian or African-Brazilian usage in the late-19th-c. Brazilian agricultural register). The standard São Paulo / Minas Gerais commercial term is *mandioquinha* or *batata-baroa* depending on region.#footnote[Brazilian Portuguese plant nomenclature: Houaiss, _Dicionário Houaiss_; standard Brazilian agricultural and ethnobotanical literature (e.g. EMBRAPA Hortaliças, _Cultivo de Mandioquinha-salsa_). The mandioca / mandioquinha folk-naming confusion is documented; the two plants are botanically unrelated despite the diminutive analogy.]

*Spanish (Andean):* *Arracacha* (Colombian Spanish, Venezuelan, Ecuadorian); *racacha* (Peruvian Spanish); *zanahoria blanca* — "white carrot" (Ecuador); *apio* in Peruvian Spanish — *but this is a regional usage that conflicts with the more common Spanish "apio" = celery*; care needed. The Andean Quechua *raqacha* is the source of the Spanish.

*Quechua:* *raqacha* (the indigenous Andean name).

*English:* *Arracacha*; *Peruvian carrot* / *Peruvian parsnip*; *white carrot* — none of these have wide currency in modern English-speaking dietetic literature, since the plant is largely unknown outside Andean Latin America and Brazil. The international diaspora of the plant is minimal compared to other Andean domesticates (potato, quinoa, sweet potato, oca, ulluco).

*Sanskrit / Arabic / Latin classical / Chinese:* *Not classical in any Old World tradition.* The plant is a *post-Columbian Andean domesticate* without recognition in the Ayurvedic, Tibb, Greco-Roman, or Chinese pharmacopoeias. Modern Indian or Tibb integrative literature does not include arracacha. Some modern Asian agricultural research on arracacha as a *potential cassava-substitute* exists (Vietnamese and Chinese experimental cultivation in the 1980s–1990s), but the plant has not entered Asian classical or modern pharmacopoeial use.

== Indigenous Andean Classical Use

The indigenous Andean tradition treats *raqacha / arracacha* as a *staple cool-mountain food crop*, alongside potato, oca, ulluco, mashua, quinoa, and kiwicha. The pre-Columbian cultivation is well-attested archaeologically; the plant was a *significant indigenous staple* before potato cultivation expanded, and remains important in the *intermediate altitudes (1500–2800 m)* of the Andean valleys where the cool but frost-free conditions favor it over high-altitude potato cultivation.

*Andean traditional uses:*
- *Boiled or steamed roots* — the principal preparation; the cooked root is sweet, fragrant (with celery-like Apiaceae aroma), with a dense potato-like texture.
- *Soups and stews* — arracacha is added to soup preparations (Colombian *sancocho*, Ecuadorian *locro de zanahoria blanca*).
- *Mashed preparation* — Colombian *mazamorra de arracacha* and similar mashes; eaten by infants and the elderly as a gentle digestive food.
- *Flour and starch* — arracacha starch is used in some regional Colombian and Ecuadorian preparations, especially as a thickener and as an *easily digestible flour for infants and convalescents* — the "arracacha for babies" register is a continuing Andean folk-pediatric tradition.
- *Leaves and stems* — used as a green vegetable in some regions, with the parsley-like aroma making them suitable as a flavoring herb.

The *constitutional reading by indigenous Andean practice* — cool-mild-mountain-staple — is *intermediate* between potato (cooler-tolerant) and cassava (warmer-tolerant), with the plant occupying the *cool-intermediate altitude* ecological niche.

== Classical Chinese, Ayurvedic, and Tibb Views — Reasoned Extension Only

*No classical entries in any Old World tradition.* The reading below applies dravyaguna / Chinese / Tibb principles to mandioquinha *by extension only*, with explicit flagging.

*Chinese reading (by extension):*
- *Property:* slightly warm (微溫) to neutral (平), tracking the sweet-nutritious staple register.
- *Flavor:* sweet (甘 gān), with mild aromatic (the Apiaceae celery-parsley aroma).
- *Channels:* Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃) — the standard placement for sweet nutritious staple roots.
- *Actions (by extension):* *補中益氣 (tonifies middle and qi)*, *健脾 (strengthens Spleen)* — analogous to the placement of other sweet-root staples (yam 山藥, taro 芋頭).
- *Cautions:* damp-heat patterns; the typical caveats for heavy-sweet-starchy foods.

*Ayurvedic reading (by extension):*
- *Rasa:* madhura (sweet); slight tikta-katu from the Apiaceae aromatic.
- *Guṇa:* guru (heavy), snigdha (unctuous when cooked).
- *Vīrya:* by extension *uṣṇa* (slightly heating) to neutral.
- *Vipāka:* madhura.
- *Doṣa effect:* pacifies Vāta; pacifies Pitta moderately; aggravates Kapha at heavy intake.
- *Karma:* _balya_, _bṛṃhaṇa_ — strengthening, nourishing.

*Tibb reading (by extension):*
- *Mizāj:* hot 1°, moist 1° — by analogy with carrot (_jazar_) and parsnip-type Apiaceae roots in the Avicennan canon.
- *Afʿāl:* nutritive; mild stomachic; mild diuretic.

*All readings are reasoned extension only and are explicitly flagged as such.*

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Approximate composition (raw mandioquinha / arracacha root, per 100 g):*
- Energy ~98 kcal; Water ~74 g
- Carbohydrate ~22 g (starch predominant; small fraction sugars); fiber ~1.5 g
- Protein ~1 g; Fat ~0.3 g
- *Minerals:* potassium ~430 mg, magnesium ~25 mg, calcium ~28 mg, iron ~1 mg, manganese, copper
- *Vitamins:* vitamin C (modest), vitamin A (substantial in the yellow-orange cultivars from β-carotene content), thiamin, niacin, folate
- *Carotenoids:* β-carotene (in the yellow cultivars) — comparable to carrot per gram, though the carrot-equivalent intake per food serving is generally lower due to smaller serving size
- *Phenolics and antioxidant compounds* — modest; chlorogenic acid and related
- *Distinctive characteristics:* highly digestible starch (very low glycemic compared to cassava or potato); excellent gentle digestibility — the basis of the Andean and Brazilian use as infant food and convalescent food#footnote[Composition data: standard Brazilian nutritional databases (TACO — Tabela Brasileira de Composição de Alimentos, NEPA-UNICAMP); EMBRAPA technical literature; modern Colombian and Peruvian agronomy reviews. The exceptional digestibility of arracacha starch is well-documented in Andean and Brazilian nutritional literature.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Excellent digestibility* — the starch is unusually well-tolerated by infants, the elderly, and convalescents; the canonical Brazilian and Andean infant-food register has clear nutritional basis.
- *Modest nutrient density* — comparable to potato in macronutrient profile; lower in starch than cassava (less calorie-dense); higher in carotenoids than potato (in the yellow cultivars).
- *No documented pharmacological actives at any significant level.*

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Culinary register:* unrestricted at normal dietary intake.
- *Allergy / contraindication:* none commonly reported; Apiaceae allergy is rare but possible.
- *Pregnancy:* safe; the gentle digestibility makes arracacha a *traditionally recommended Brazilian convalescent and pregnancy food*.
- *Infants:* canonically recommended as a *first-food* in Brazilian and Andean infant-feeding tradition — gentle, low-allergen, digestible, palatable.
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.*

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

Arracacha / mandioquinha is a *cool-tropical-highland* crop — native to the *cool intermediate altitudes of the tropical Andes (1500–2800 m)*, where the air temperature is cool-mild year-round (no winter freeze; no excessive summer heat). The plant is *not* suited to the lowland tropics (too hot) or the high Andean páramo (too cold), occupying the *intermediate-altitude tropical highland* ecological niche. Modern Brazilian cultivation is in *highland Minas Gerais, Espírito Santo, São Paulo, Paraná, and Santa Catarina*, replicating the cool-intermediate-altitude conditions. See `climates.typ` for the foundational framework (the "tropical highland" zone).

=== Tropical highland (native climate)

The cool-mild-tropical-mountain pattern: cool year-round; modest seasonal variation; sufficient rainfall; rich volcanic soils (in the Andean and Brazilian highland regions). Constitutional pressure: mild Vāta from the cool air and high UV exposure; minimal Pitta or Kapha pressure.

*Traditional preparation.* The Andean and Brazilian traditional preparations are *boiled / steamed / mashed* — the gentle cooking preserves the digestibility and aromatic quality. *Caldo de mandioquinha* (Brazilian arracacha soup), often with chicken or vegetable broth, is the canonical Brazilian comfort-food preparation. *Mandioquinha frita* (fried) is the modern São Paulo restaurant rendering.

*Cares.* The arracacha is *climatically and constitutionally apt for the cool-tropical-highland zone where it grows*; the gentle-digestibility character makes it widely transposable across climates and constitutional types — a *forgiving food* with few contraindications. The modern Brazilian use across all regions (including the hot lowland) is climatically wider than the strict classical-constitutional reading would predict, but the food's gentle profile makes the transposition generally well-tolerated.

=== Modern transposition

Modern Brazilian cuisine uses mandioquinha across all national climates — a post-classical extension that has worked well due to the food's mild digestibility. International spread is minimal; the plant remains essentially regional. See `climates.typ` for context.

